&lt;B&gt;EORS_FOI&lt;/B&gt;&lt;BR&gt;&#x0D;
Isokinetic knee testing was conducted in the seated position using a Cybex isokinetic dynamometer (CSMI, Stoughton, MA).  Single leg knee extensor and flexor strength (peak torque) was assessed by having subjects perform five maximal concentric knee extension / flexion repetitions at 60 degrees per second.  The primary dependent variables for this test were peak torque (Nm).&#x0D;
&lt;P&gt;&#x0D;
&lt;b&gt;EORS_MARES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&#x0D;
Testing was conducted using a Cybex isokinetic dynamometer (HUMAC NORM).  During each session, subjects were asked to perform concentric muscle contractions as they moved the lever arm of the isokinetic testing device.  However, the device controlled their speed of movement.  Subjects participated in concentric knee extension and flexion at a slow speed and concentric knee extension and flexion at a fast speed as a test of muscle endurance.&#x0D;
&lt;P&gt;&#x0D;
&lt;b&gt;EORS_TIMED&lt;/b&gt;&lt;BR&gt;&#x0D;
Isokinetic strength was completed using an isokinetic testing protocol. Knee extensor and plantar flexor strength were evaluated. Several warm-up repetitions were performed at submaximal (~40%1RM) intensity alternating between extension and flexion. Two warm-up repetitions were performed at a higher intensity alternating between extension and flexion. Five testing repetitions were performed at maximal intensity for extension and flexion.&#x0D;
&lt;P&gt;&#x0D;
&lt;b&gt;MR079G&lt;/B&gt;&lt;BR&gt;&#x0D;
Isokinetic Dynamometer (HUMAC) system was an exercise device used clinically by physicians and physical therapists to rehabilitate and test athletes and persons with injury.  This isokinetic device controlled the speed at which a person moved their limb and measured the torque developed when the person tries to move faster than the machine will allow.  Muscle performance testing was administered using an isokinetic dynamometer on selected muscle groups of the knee, ankle, and trunk.&#x0D;
&lt;P&gt;&#x0D;
&lt;b&gt;BRSMFSR&lt;/B&gt;&lt;BR&gt;&#x0D;
Testing was conducted using a Cybex isokinetic dynamometer (HUMAC NORM).  A dorsiflexion torque was then applied at 250o/sec to randomized angular amplitudes of 5, 10, and 15 degrees.  Six trials were conducted at each of the three amplitudes.  For half of these trials, EMG data on the FSR was obtained by instructing subjects to resist the force.  For the EMG Setup, electrodes were placed in the following locations on the right and left legs: the tibialus medial, tibialus lateral and the gastrocnemius tendon insertion, belly.&#x0D;
&lt;end&gt;; &lt;B&gt;SkyMusc&lt;/B&gt;&lt;BR&gt;&#x0D;
The Skylab Cybex Isokinetic Dynamometer is a device capable of measuring muscle strength that was utilized pre- and postflight to test all Skylab program crews.  The device may be rotated in either direction without resistance until an adjustable limit speed is reached.  Speed cannot be increased above this limit by forces of any magnitude.  Muscle forces are continuously recorded.  Various arms, handles and attachments may be fitted to the dynamometer to couple any desired segment of the body to the machine.&#x0D;
&lt;P&gt;&#x0D;
During Skylab, crewmen used the device preflight and postflight as follows:  after a thorough warm up, ten maximum effort full flexions and extensions of the arm at the elbow and of the hip and knee at an angular rate of 45 degrees per second were made.&#x0D;
&lt;P&gt;&#x0D;
A continuous force record was made of each repetition at a rate of 25 millimeters per second and the integral of force, or under these conditions, work, was recorded on a second channel.&#x0D;
&lt;P&gt;&#x0D;
Machine errors were small, 2 to 3 percent or less.  The tests provided a measurement of strength comparable to the more commonly used isometric testing, but had the advantage of recording this force throughout the whole range of motion, as well as allowing a number of repetitions for statistical purposes.  It was sensitive enough to show small changes in performance that occur over the course of several days.&lt;END&gt;

